    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d886">

                <pb facs="1058" n="1048" sameAs="frus:frus1943v01/pg1048"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">800.515/912: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        American Representative to the French Committee of National Liberation at
                        Algiers</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Wilson</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1943-12-11T23:00:00-04:00">December 11,
                            1943—11 p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>124. Your 115, December 9, 11 a.m. The British on November 22 proposed a change
                    in the formula of October 6<anchor n="34" xml:id="d886-f1"/> which had been
                    agreed to between the United States and British Governments for French
                    participation in Civil Affairs in metropolitan France, and as the new formula
                    proposed more direct dealing with the French Committee of National Liberation
                    the matter was referred to the President. The President stated that all
                    consideration of Civil Affairs for France should be held in abeyance until his
                        return<anchor n="35" xml:id="d886-f2"/> and further consideration.</p>
                <p>In dealing with currency for military operation, War Department, with concurrence
                    of State Department, has taken the position that as there is no governmental
                    authority in existence for issuance of French <pb facs="1059" n="1049"
                        xml:id="pg1049"/> currency, an Allied military currency is the only form
                    which could be legally used. That is the way the matter now stands. It is hoped
                    that the form of currency to be used will be one which will not be objectionable
                    to the French people.</p>
                <p>We hope to be in a position soon to give you definite information in the
                    premises. In the meantime, it is impossible for any of us to discuss these
                    matters further.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d886-f1" n="34" xml:id="d886-f1-content">
                <p>See letter of October 7 from Mr. H. D. White, <ref target="#pg1044">p.
                    1044</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d886-f2" n="35" xml:id="d886-f2-content">
                <p>President Roosevelt returned on December 11 from meetings with Marshal Stalin,
                    Prime Minister Churchill, President Inönü of Turkey, and Generalissimo Chiang
                    Kai-shek. For documentation on these conferences, see <ref
                        target="frus1943CairoTehran">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, The Conferences at Cairo and
                        Tehran, 1943</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
